Moth keeps the source language small. Compiler complexity is acceptable when it
makes the programmer-facing model simpler, safer and more predictable. Keep
source behaviour statically resolved and locally inspectable. Hide backend
optimisation behind stable semantics so implementation techniques do not become
source-language obligations.

### Central bias

- keep the source language small, explicit and statically inspectable
- prefer constrained built-in mechanisms over general metaprogramming
- prefer static resolution over runtime dynamism
- keep backend optimisations hidden behind stable source semantics
- avoid features that need pervasive solver-heavy reasoning

### Preferred constrained mechanisms

[table:
    [th:
        [: Need]
        [: Preferred mechanism]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Compile-time generation]
        [: Templates, constants and compiler- or builder-owned directives]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Static reuse]
        [: Generic functions and explicit trait bounds]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Runtime heterogeneity]
        [: Choices]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Expected failure]
        [: `Error!`, postfix propagation and `catch`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Invariant failure]
        [: `assert`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Explicit result-like domain values]
        [: Ordinary choices]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Platform integration]
        [: Builder packages, external packages and source-backed packages expose stable capability semantics. Project builders and backends resolve those semantics to platform-specific artefacts.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Memory safety]
        [: Mandatory borrow validation plus mandatory lifetime-topology validation. Static proof is the baseline; a garbage-collected representation is one permitted physical representation, never the correctness mechanism.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Mutation]
        [: Explicit exclusive access rather than implicit mutation]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Ownership]
        [: Compiler-inferred affine cleanup transfer rather than source-visible move or lifetime syntax]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Release memory]
        [: Backends that advertise full memory control lower release builds without a tracing collector. This is a backend capability, not a source or project mode.]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Declared bulk lifetimes]
        [: Accepted deferred `name:` / `into name` hard lifetime regions]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Performance]
        [: Hidden compiler and backend optimisation]
    ]
]

### Platform-agnostic source and builder ownership

Source expresses stable semantics rather than target identity. Source cannot
inspect the OS, architecture, backend or physical target, and cannot condition
imports, declarations, exports or bodies on target identity. Ordinary `$config`
values and static `if` are not structural selection mechanisms: they cannot
remove declarations or dependencies. Strict `$project` metadata and explicit
builder capabilities likewise do not expose physical target identity.

Future `$feature` is the separate structural direction. It may select source
structure before graph and declaration publication using bootstrap-known feature
data and the high-level selected-builder identity. Known feature names must be
distinguished from enabled features so a typo cannot silently disable code; its
predicate, nesting and config-declaration grammar belongs to a future plan.

Builders cannot expose target identity as pseudo-config values. Builder identity
is not physical target identity. A selected builder may expose stable capability
APIs, compiler target validation rejects unsupported reachable capability use,
and backends realise builder-owned target partition and output policy. This is
intentional outside-scope policy, not merely deferred syntax.

### Directive and representation boundaries

Compiler-known general directives are an accepted direction for language and
structural semantics. Arbitrary user macros and builder directives that
transform AST are excluded; constrained builder directives may provide
templates, metadata and configuration without rewriting language semantics.

Excluded source regions will not resolve their contained builder directives
against the active directive surface. They still need a sound structural
boundary. A future plan must address directive-controlled template lexing
before arbitrary unknown syntax can be skipped.

Future `$layout` constrains representation while preserving nominal identity.
C order alone is not a complete ABI contract, and SoA is not automatically a
scalar layout option. Future `$export` replaces rather than permanently
duplicates the current export-block syntax; whether it supports a directive
block remains undecided.

`$test` is a deferred root-purpose direction; its runner, command and
lifecycle details are undefined. A separate custom-metadata directive remains
open design, so `$project` stays strict in the meantime.

### Design review questions

- Does this introduce a new source category when an existing mechanism is enough?
- Does it make behaviour more implicit?
- Does it need whole-program or solver-heavy reasoning?
- Does it introduce erased runtime machinery?
- Does it leak backend representation?
- Does it expose optimisation mechanics?
- Is it accepted but unimplemented, undecided or intentionally excluded?
- Does it make code harder to inspect locally?
- Does this let source observe or branch on a physical target, backend or runtime platform that should remain builder-owned?

### LLM-aware design

Coding agents are increasingly becoming a part of coding workflows. 

Moth is designed to be strict and provide clear and fast feedback to coding agents.

Developers can focus more on high level design, architecture or important implementation detailed, while agents handle the busy work.

Good languages for the future should be easy to review and strict about what the code should look like and how it's structured.
This means developers can worry less about whether their code is reliable / memory safe / structured correctly / using the correct patterns or idioms.

A small syntax, strict rules, opinionated design, fast tooling and good diagnostics give agents less space to make mistakes or drift from ideomatic code.

Compiler diagnostics should carry stable codes, structured facts and source metadata for editors, development servers and coding agents.

Moth has very little training data, but this may be useful later: 
examples can grow around the language that exists and agents will have to follow your codebase style more strictly. 
No legacy patterns to pollute generated output.

See @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/HUMANS.md (HUMANS.md) for more general stances and opinions on getting good output out of LLMs.

